    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d872">

                <pb facs="1169" n="1049" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg1049"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">837.51/879</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Cuban Chargé</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Padró</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-11-04"><hi rendition="#italic">November 4,
                        1922</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your note of the 3rd instant, in which you inform me that your Government
                    authorizes you to state that in its opinion the taxes especially allocated to
                    the service of the loan mentioned in your note of October 18, 1922, are
                    sufficient and, at the same time, to state that should they for any reasons not
                    be sufficient the President of Cuba will pledge additional funds from the
                    ordinary revenues of the Cuban Government to the service of the loan, in
                    accordance with the authority granted to him by the loan statute.</p>
                <p>In view of this assurance I take pleasure in informing you that the Department
                    has no objection to the negotiation by the Cuban Government of the loan
                    described in your note under acknowledgment.<anchor n="56" xml:id="d872-f1"
                    /></p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d872-f1" n="56" xml:id="d872-f1-content">
                <p>At the same time the Acting Secretary informed the Chargé orally that the
                    Government of the United States viewed with great concern the pending amnesty
                    bill as well as the possibility of any changes in the existing Cabinet. The
                    amnesty bill became a law June 5, 1924.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
